<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Balša II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1385">1385</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balsic-noble-family">Balšić noble family</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Balša II was a lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeta-region">Zeta</a> of the Balšić family, which took control of the land around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-skadar">Lake Skadar</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Stefan Dušan</a>'s death. He ruled Zeta from 1378 and expanded into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>, gaining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlore">Valona</a> by marriage and seizing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Durrës</a> in 1385. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-thopia">Karl Thopia</a> called in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, who defeated and killed him at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-savra">Battle of Savra</a> that year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/balsa-ii.jpg" alt="Balša II" width="320" height="553" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Petarrc13 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bal%C5%A1i%C4%87_noble_family_banner.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Balša II was the youngest of the three sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balsa-i">Balša I</a>, his brothers being Stracimir and Đurađ I. According to Mavro Orbini, the elder Balša was a petty nobleman who held a single village near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-skadar">Lake Skadar</a> under the Serbian Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Dušan the Mighty</a>. During the weak rule of Emperor Uroš V that followed, the father and his sons gained power in Lower Zeta, once the lands of gospodin Žarko, and then turned on Upper Zeta, murdering its holder Đuraš Ilijić. Orbini described Balša II as good-natured and an accomplished horseman, but not of great mind.</p><p>The family rose from petty nobility to provincial lords and became powerful after 1362. In the summer of 1364 the brothers were defeated in a skirmish by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-thopia">Karl Thopia</a>, who held Đurađ prisoner until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a> mediated a peace in 1366. In 1372 Balša married either Komnina, daughter of the Despot John Komnenos Asen, or, as the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muzaka-family">Muzaka family</a> chronicle claimed, Comita, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrea-ii-muzaka">Andrea II Muzaka</a>. Either way, the marriage secured him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlore">Vlorë</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berat">Berat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanine">Kaninë</a> by right of his wife.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Balša came to power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeta-region">Zeta</a> on 13 January 1378, after the death of his elder brother Đurađ I. His authority was felt only around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkoder">Scutari</a> and on the eastern part of Zeta's coast, and the Đurašević-Crnojević family, allies of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and sworn enemies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balsic-noble-family">Balšići</a>, refused to recognise him. In 1385 he went to war for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Durazzo</a> and took it at the fourth attempt, which gave him the right to add the title of duke to his title of gospodin, or lord.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-thopia">Karl Thopia</a> appealed to the Ottoman sultan Murat I, and an Ottoman army under Hajruddin Pasha routed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balsic-noble-family">Balšići</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-savra">Battle of Savra</a>, on the Saurian field near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lushnje">Lushnjë</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> beheaded Balša, who died on 18 September 1385, and his head was sent to Hajruddin Pasha. The family's rule over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Durazzo</a> ended with him.</p><p>His widow and their daughter Ruđina took over his lands in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>, and the widow seems to have been the main ruler of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlore">Valona</a> until her death in 1396, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muzaka-family">Muzaka family</a> gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berat">Berat</a>. In 1391 Ruđina married Mrkša Žarković, who ruled Valona until 1414, and she then held it herself until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> seized the city and its citadel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanine">Kanina</a> in 1417. She fled to Corfu, and her nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balsa-iii">Balša III</a>, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeta-region">Zeta</a>, made her governor of Budva.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Balša II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Balša II</b> seized Durrës in 1385, and Karl called in the Ottomans, who killed Balša at Savra; Karl recovered the city soon after.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-albania-medieval">Principality of Albania (medieval)</a></li><li><blockquote>After John's son Alexander, the city passed to <b>Balša II</b> of Zeta under the Balšići around 1372, and then through his widow Komita to her son-in-law Merksha Žarković and, after his death in 1414, to his widow Ruđina.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-valona">Principality of Valona</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1356, after Stefan Dušan's death, Balša I and his sons Stracimir, Đurađ I and <b>Balša II</b> took control of Zeta, the land around Lake Skadar and the Gulf of Kotor, ruling from Skadar (Shkodër).</blockquote><a href="/state/zeta-under-the-balsici">Zeta under the Balšići</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Principality of Zeta, in what is now <a href="/countries/albania">Albania</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myzeqe">Myzeqe</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/albania">Albania</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.07,19.5&amp;year=1375" aria-label="Principality of Zeta on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Balša II"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4337 3945h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4337 3945h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.07,19.5&amp;year=1375">What was here in 1375? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
